<RecruiterLayout
  title="Approved / user research into product definition"
  description="Twenty user interviews turned into a defined product, a sized market, a pricing model and a channel plan for a restaurant discovery app."
  heading="approved."
  course="Marketing"
  year="2022"
  scope="team project, four people"
  tools={['User interviews', 'Persona development', 'Market sizing', 'Competitor analysis', 'Pricing strategy', 'Marketing mix']}
>

  <h2>the brief</h2>

  <p>
    Take a product from nothing to a defined proposition: find a real problem, size who has it, work out what to
    build, what to charge and how to reach them.
  </p>

  <h2>approach</h2>

  <h3>Start with the problem, not the idea</h3>
  <p>
    We interviewed <strong>20 people</strong> about how they choose a restaurant. The questions were about friction,
    not preferences, which is what stopped the answers being a list of favourite places.
  </p>
  <p>Two problems came up repeatedly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No time to cook, because of work and pace of life</li>
    <li>No single place to compare menus and prices before deciding</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The second one is the interesting one. It is not a restaurant problem, it is an information problem, which means
    software can actually solve it.
  </p>

  <h3>Define and size the audience</h3>
  <p>
    Target population: employed women aged 25 to 55, living in urban areas, comfortable with technology and
    carrying a smartphone. Sized at <strong>188,830 employed women</strong> using national statistics office data on
    ICT usage, so the figure came from a source rather than from us.
  </p>
  <p>
    We wrote that up as a persona, Sandra, 33, a PR manager in New Belgrade with two small children and a food
    budget of 35,000 RSD a month. The detail that mattered was not her job title, it was that she cares about
    feeding her children well but has almost no time to research where.
  </p>

  <h3>Position against what already exists</h3>
  <p>
    Three competitors, each assessed on where it falls short for this specific user: TripAdvisor spreads across
    hotels, shopping and travel, Google Reviews is hard to navigate for this purpose and under-used, and Restorani
    Beograd covers only Belgrade. That gap defined what the product needed to be: fast search over a restaurant
    database, filtering by criteria, ranked results, reviews of both existing and new places.
  </p>

  <h3>Price it</h3>
  <p>
    Penetration pricing. The app itself is free, with a premium tier at 999 RSD monthly, 3,999 for six months, or
    6,999 for a year, covering ad-free use and guaranteed table reservation, plus 5% off at partner restaurants.
    Free entry to build the database of reviews, paid tier for the people who use it most.
  </p>

  <h2>what came out of it</h2>

  <div class="rc-result">
    <p class="rc-result-label">the through-line</p>
    <p>
      Every downstream decision traces back to the interviews. The two problems set the product, the product set the
      pricing model, and the audience definition set the channels: radio in the 3pm to 6pm slot, public transport
      posters, sponsored placements on food sites, and paid work with two named influencers whose audience matched
      the persona.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Distribution across Google Play, App Store and Huawei AppGallery, under the line "vreme uštedi, sebe poštedi",
    save time, spare yourself.
  </p>

  <p class="rc-note">
    Team project with three colleagues. I was involved across all sections rather than owning one, so I know the
    reasoning behind each part of it, including the parts I did not write first.
  </p>

</RecruiterLayout>
